**THEOREM 7.4 (The iterator theorem).** *The combinators* $\mathbf{Z}_n$ *enjoy the following functionality property which holds for all equivalence relations* $A$:

(i) $\mathbf{Z}_n : (A \to A) \to (A \to A)$.

*Further, if any element* $z \in \mathbf{P}\omega$ *satisfies (i) for all* $A$, *then it must be one of the iterators, provided that* $z(f) = z(\lambda x.\, f(x))$ *holds for all* $f \in \mathbf{P}\omega$.

That each of the $\mathbf{Z}_n$ satisfies Theorem 7.4(i) is obvious. Suppose $z$ were another such element. Then clearly:

$$z = \lambda f \lambda x.\, z(f)(x).$$

Suppose $f$ and $x$ are fixed for the moment. Let:

$$A = \{(f^n(x), f^n(x)) \mid n \in \omega\},$$

where we can suppose in addition that:

$$f = \lambda x.\, f(x).$$

Then $f : A \to A$ is clear, and so $z(f) : A \to A$ also. But $x : A$, therefore $z(f)(x) = f^n(x)$, for some $n \in \omega$, because $z(f)(x) : A$. The trouble with this easy part of the argument is that the integer $n$ depends on $f$ and $x$. What we must show is that it is independent of $f$ and $x$; then $z = \mathbf{Z}_n$ will follow.

Plotkin's method for this case is to introduce some independent successor functions:

$$(7.17) \quad \sigma_j = \lambda x.\, \{(j, k + 1) \mid (j, k) \in x\}.$$

Note that:

$$(7.18) \quad \sigma_j^m((j', 0)) = \begin{cases} (j, m) & \text{if } j = j'; \\ \bot & \text{if } j \neq j'. \end{cases}$$

It then follows that:

$$(7.19) \quad (\sigma_j \cup \sigma_{j'})^m((j, 0) \cup (j', 0)) = (j, m) \cup (j', m).$$

Having these identities, we return to the argument. From what we saw before, given $j \in \omega$, there is an $n_j$ such that:

$$z(\sigma_j)((j, 0)) = \sigma_j^{n_j}((j, 0)) = (j, n_j)$$

Take any two $j, j' \in \omega$. We also know there is an $n \in \omega$ where:

$$z(\sigma_j \cup \sigma_{j'})((j, 0) \cup (j', 0)) = (j, n) \cup (j', n).$$

<!-- page 46 -->

in view of (7.19). But since $\sigma_j \subseteq \sigma_j \cup \sigma_{j'}$ and $\sigma_{j'} \subseteq \sigma_j \cup \sigma_{j'}$, we have:

$$(j, n_j) \cup (j', n_{j'}) \subseteq (j, n) \cup (j', n).$$

It follows that

$$n_j = n = n_{j'},$$

and so they are all equal. This determines the fixed $n \in \omega$ we want.

Suppose that both $f$ and $x$ are finite sets in $\mathbf{P}\omega$. Choose $j > \max(f \cup x)$. Let $A$ this time be the least equivalence relation such that:

$$f^m(x) \, A \, f^m(x) \cup (j, m)$$

holds for all $m \in \omega$. We then check that:

$$\lambda x.\, f(x) \, (A \to A) \, (\lambda x.\, f(x)) \cup \sigma_j.$$

Therefore, we have:

$$z(\lambda x.\, f(x)) \, (A \to A) \, z((\lambda x.\, f(x)) \cup \sigma_j),$$

and since $x \, A \, x \cup (j, 0)$, we get:

$$z(\lambda x.\, f(x))(x) \, A \, z((\lambda x.\, f(x)) \cup \sigma_j)(x \cup (j, 0)).$$

Now there is an integer $m \in \omega$ such that:

$$
\begin{aligned}
z((\lambda x.\, f(x)) \cup \sigma_j)(x \cup (j, 0)) &= f^m(x) \cup \sigma_j^m((j, 0)) \\
&= f^m(x) \cup (j, m),
\end{aligned}
$$

where we have been able to separate $f$ and $\sigma$ because $j$ is so large. But the right-hand side must contain $z(\sigma_j)(j, 0) = (j, n)$. Thus $m$ and our fixed $n$ are the same. The other element

$$z(\lambda x.\, f(x))(x) = f^q(x)$$

for some $q \in \omega$. Thus we have:

$$f^q(x) \, A \, f^n(x) \cup (j, n).$$

Again since $j$ is so large, $(j, n) \notin f^q(x)$. Thus by our choice of $A$ we must have $f^q(x) = f^n(x)$. This means then, since $n$ is fixed, that for *all finite* $f, x$:

$$z(\lambda x.\, f(x))(x) = f^n(x).$$

But then by continuity this equation holds for *all* $f, x$. It follows now that $z = \mathbf{Z}_n$, by the proviso of the theorem.

These results bring up many questions which we leave unanswered here. For example, which combinators (i.e., pure $\lambda$-terms) have functionality as in the examples above, and can we decide when a term is one such? In particular, can the diagonal combinator $\lambda x.\, x(x)$ be typed? (The argument of Hindley, et al. (1972, p. 81) is purely formal and does not apparently apply to the model.) What about terms in LAMBDA beyond the pure $\lambda$-calculus?

<!-- page 47 -->

<!-- formalization -->

**Formalization.** The iterator theorem packages Plotkin’s tag-erasure argument: successor identities for $\sigma_j$, a restricted equivalence that pairs finite orbits with fresh tags, and uniqueness of any $\eta$-normalized $z$ that is functional at every $(A\to A)\to(A\to A)$.

**Definition (`eraseTag`).** Erase tag $j$ from $u$ by discarding every pair whose first component is $j$.

**Theorem (`eraseTag.Z_finite`).** On a finite orbit $Z_m(e_p,e_q)$, erasing the fresh tag $\max(p,q)+1$ leaves the orbit unchanged.

*Proof.* Finite orbits never carry that fresh tag. ∎

**Theorem (`eraseTag.Z_finite_union`).** Erasing the fresh tag from an orbit union a fresh singleton recovers the plain orbit.

*Proof.* Membership either lies in the orbit (kept) or is the fresh pair (discarded). ∎

**Definition (`plotkinGood`).** An element is Plotkin-good when it is a finite orbit of $e_p$ on $e_q$, or that orbit union the fresh singleton at step $m$.

**Definition (`plotkinRel`).** The generating relation identifies an orbit with the same orbit union its fresh singleton at the same step, in either order.

**Definition (`plotkinE`).** The Plotkin equivalence relates good elements whose equivalence closure under that generating relation identifies them.

**Theorem (`plotkinE.erase`).** Related elements have equal erase-tag projections at the fresh tag.

*Proof.* Induction on the generated equivalence, using the two erase-tag orbit identities. ∎

**Theorem (`plotkinE.finite_separation`).** Iterating the $\eta$-normalized finite graph of $e_p$ united with $\sigma_j$ on $e_q$ union a fresh zero yields the finite orbit union the fresh singleton.

*Proof.* Induction on the iterate, separating $e_p$ from $\sigma_j$ by freshness of the tag. ∎

**Theorem (`plotkinE.function_related`).** The $\eta$-normalized graph of $e_p$ is related in $A\to A$ to that graph united with $\sigma_j$, for $A$ the Plotkin equivalence at the fresh tag.

*Proof.* Both sides are graphs; on related arguments, erase-tag agreement and the four orbit/singleton cases produce related results. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_7_18_eq`).** $\sigma_j^m(\{(j,0)\})=\{(j,m)\}$.

*Proof.* Induction, applying the successor-on-singleton law for $\sigma_j$. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_7_18_ne`).** If $j\neq j'$, then $\sigma_j^{m+1}(\{(j',0)\})=\bot$.

*Proof.* Induction: the first step kills a wrong tag, and $\sigma_j$ preserves $\bot$. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_7_19`).** $(\sigma_j\cup\sigma_{j'})^m(\{(j,0)\}\cup\{(j',0)\})=\{(j,m)\}\cup\{(j',m)\}$.

*Proof.* Equal tags reduce to the previous identity; distinct tags induct using the pairwise successor law. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4.i`).** Each iterator $\mathbf{Z}_n$ is a member of $(A\to A)\to(A\to A)$ for every restricted equivalence $A$.

*Proof.* Iterators are graphs, and relatedness is preserved by the iterate law for $Z$. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4.plotkin`).** If $z$ is $\eta$-normalized and functional at every $(A\to A)\to(A\to A)$, then for any $f$ and $x$ there is an $n$ with $z(f)(x)=f^n(x)$.

*Proof.* Specialize to the iterate equivalence of the $\eta$-normalized $f$ at $x$; functionality forces the value into that orbit. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4.finite`).** Under those hypotheses, if $z(\sigma_j)(\{(j,0)\})=\{(j,n)\}$ for every $j$, then on finite $e_p$ and $e_q$ one has $z(e_p)(e_q)=e_p^n(e_q)$.

*Proof.* Relate the $\eta$-normalized finite graph to its union with a fresh $\sigma_j$ in the Plotkin equivalence; compare orbits by erase-tag, and force the iterate index to equal the fixed $n$ by a fresh-tag witness. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4.sigma_index`).** Under those hypotheses there is a single $n$ such that $z(\sigma_j)(\{(j,0)\})=\{(j,n)\}$ for every $j$.

*Proof.* Compare the indices at $0$, at $j$, and at their union via (7.18)–(7.19) and monotonicity of application. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4.unique`).** Under those hypotheses, $z=\mathbf{Z}_n$ for that $n$.

*Proof.* On finite arguments the previous two tools give agreement with $\mathbf{Z}_n$; continuity and $\eta$-normalization extend to all arguments, then graph extensionality. ∎

**Theorem (`theorem_7_4`).** Every $\mathbf{Z}_n$ is functional at all $(A\to A)\to(A\to A)$, and every $\eta$-normalized $z$ with that functionality is some $\mathbf{Z}_n$.

*Proof.* Assemble the membership and uniqueness tools. ∎
